{"name":"Ragtime Blues","hex":"#4a5e6c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ragtime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A musical form having a rhythm characterized by strong syncopation in the melody with a regularly accented accompaniment.","A piece of music in this style."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To perform this style of music."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ragtime"},{"term":"blues","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of blue"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feeling of sadness or depression.","One's particular life experience, particularly including the hardships one has faced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blues"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ragtime","blocks":[{"text":"Ragtime, also spelled rag-time or rag time, is a musical style noted for its syncopated or \"ragged\" rhythm. It originated in African American communities in the late 19th century and was propelled to popularity in the 1890s to 1910s by composers such as James Scott, Joseph Lamb, and particularly Scott Joplin. Known as the \"King of Ragtime\", Joplin gained fame through compositions like \"Maple Leaf Rag\" and \"The Entertainer\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Maple_Leaf_Rag.PNG/330px-Maple_Leaf_Rag.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ragtime"},{"title":"Blues","blocks":[{"text":"Blues is a music genre and musical form that originated among African Americans in the Deep South of the United States around the 1860s. Blues has incorporated spirituals, work songs, field hollers, shouts, chants, and rhymed simple narrative ballads from the African-American culture. The blues form is ubiquitous in jazz, rhythm and blues, and rock and roll, and is characterized by the call-and-response pattern, the blues scale, and specific chord progressions, of which the twelve-bar blues is the most common. Blue notes, usually thirds, fifths or sevenths flattened in pitch, are also an essential part of the sound. Blues shuffles or walking bass reinforce the trance-like rhythm and form a repetitive effect known as the groove."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blues"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Las_ma_la.jpg/500px-Las_ma_la.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Las_ma_la.jpg/500px-Las_ma_la.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cover for \"La Pas Ma La\" sheet music (1895). Words and music by Ernest Hogan.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALas_ma_la.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Scott_Joplin_in_1912.jpg/500px-Scott_Joplin_in_1912.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Scott_Joplin_in_1912.jpg/500px-Scott_Joplin_in_1912.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Scott Joplin, considered the \"King of Ragtime\" (1912)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AScott_Joplin_in_1912.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/The_Top_Liner_Rag.jpg/330px-The_Top_Liner_Rag.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/The_Top_Liner_Rag.jpg/330px-The_Top_Liner_Rag.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Joseph Lamb's 1916 \"The Top Liner Rag\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Top_Liner_Rag.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Chase_%26_Baker_player_piano%2C_Buffalo%2C_NY%2C_circa_1885.JPG/500px-Chase_%26_Baker_player_piano%2C_Buffalo%2C_NY%2C_circa_1885.JPG?utm_so